    "case_id": "598",
    "case_summary": "A 35-year-old man was brought to the emergency department unconscious, with a Glasgow Coma Scale of 3. According to emergency medical services, the patient was found unconscious after smoking substances. Medical history includes polysubstance use for the past 10 years, including vapor inhalation via tin foil and pipe use. Patient has a 15 pack-year smoking history of tobacco. No family history available as patient was adopted.\n\nPatient was initially unconscious with Glasgow Coma Scale of 3. After recovery and chest tube placement, patient was observed ambulating the floors of the unit without respiratory distress. Initially required 15L oxygen on non-rebreather mask, later weaned to room air with normal oxygen saturation.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - Alpha-1 antitrypsin level: Normal\n  - HIV rapid antibody test: Nonreactive\n  - Urine drug screen: Positive for methamphetamines and opiates\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  - Initial chest radiograph: Right lateral and basilar pneumothorax, approximately 30% in volume\n  - CT angiography of chest (with and without contrast): Severe emphysema and bullous changes throughout the lungs, left lung lobe greater than right. Large bulla at posterior left lower lung measuring almost 18 cm. Marked hyperinflated lungs with coarse interstitial thickening and scarring. No evidence of pulmonary embolism.\n  - CT imaging of head and cervical spine without contrast: Negative\n\n  Image Descriptions:\n  Figure 1: Severe emphysema and bullous changes throughout the patient's lungs, left greater than right, with a large bulla at the posterior left lower lobe measuring up to 18 cm\n  Figure 2: Severe emphysema and bullous changes throughout the patient's lungs with large bullae",
